    "scenario": "IP Overlay GRE Keepalive and MTU Adjustment",
    "goals": "• {{router2_name}} is a transit provider router. {{router1_name}} and {{router3_name}} are two customer sites that only reach each other through it, and each has a default route towards {{router2_name}}:\n    {{router1_name}}: public address {{link1_ip1}} on {{link1_cidr}}, private network {{router1_loopback1_cidr}} on Loopback1\n    {{router3_name}}: public address {{link2_ip2}} on {{link2_cidr}}, private network {{router3_loopback1_cidr}} on Loopback1\n• {{router2_name}} only knows its two directly connected public subnets and must never learn the private networks.\n• A GRE tunnel Tunnel{{tunnel_id}} between {{router1_name}} and {{router3_name}} is already up over the transit, addressed out of {{tunnel_cidr}}, and OSPF process 1 area 0 already runs over it and carries both private networks.\n• Two problems remain. First, the tunnel interface stays up as long as its own source interface is up, even when the remote end has disappeared, so OSPF keeps using a black hole. Second, large packets sent into the tunnel are dropped once the GRE overhead is added.\n• Make the tunnel detect a dead remote end on both routers, by probing the far end every {{keepalive_interval}} seconds and declaring the tunnel down after {{keepalive_retries}} unanswered probes.\n• Set the IP MTU of the tunnel to {{tunnel_mtu}} bytes on both routers, and clamp the TCP maximum segment size of sessions crossing the tunnel to {{tcp_mss}} bytes so that TCP connections are not black-holed.\n• Do not change the tunnel addressing, the tunnel source or destination, or the OSPF configuration: the adjacency must stay up and a {{ping_size}} byte packet must still get through between the two private networks.",
